91986083984375, 0.03594970703125, -0.0009255409240722656, 0.017425537109375, -0.004795074462890625, 0.0083160400390625, -0.029449462890625, 0.0321044921875, -0.017730712890625, -0.0011243820190429688, -0.033966064453125, -0.02581787109375, -0.0125579833984375, -0.021148681640625, 0.04638671875, 0.01983642578125, 0.0204010009765625, 0.0170135498046875, 0.0267486572265625, 0.006298065185546875, 0.0118865966796875, -0.051422119140625, 0.00806427001953125, -0.0012607574462890625, -0.0033550262451171875, 0.0325927734375, -0.0270538330078125, 0.01392364501953125, -0.022125244140625, -0.00879669189453125, 0.00027108192443847656, -0.0001074075698852539, 0.0164947509765625, 0.0071258544921875, 0.0009212493896484375, 0.01349639892578125, -0.01033782958984375, 0.0197906494140625, -0.01537322998046875, 0.02679443359375, -0.005176544189453125, 0.01190185546875, 0.00899505615234375, -0.018524169921875, -0.0136871337890625, -0.0079193115234375, -0.0263214111328125, -0.0133209228515625, -0.00905609130859375, 0.01442718505859375, -0.0457763671875, 0.021026611328125, 0.006534576416015625, 0.0174560546875, -0.0083465576171875, -0.0094146728515625, -0.0199737548828125, 0.0108489990234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. In this context, we define **level-0 actions** as the actual choices that a player makes when instructed to act as a level-0 player\u2014that is, to make a decision without any strategic consideration of others\u2019 possible moves. In contrast, **level-0 beliefs** refer to participants\u2019 explicit predictions about what a hypothetical level-0 player (not themselves) would do in a given game. In our study, participants were asked to state what they believed a generic, non-strategic player\u2014someone playing the game for the first time and not thinking about others\u2014would choose. This distinction is important because a participant\u2019s own intuitive choice may differ from their belief about what others would do absent strategic reasoning, and we explicitly communicated this difference to participants during instructions and elicitation.\n\n**Research Questions and Hypotheses**\n\nThis study investigates two main research questions: (1) Do level-0 actions and beliefs coincide in classic experimental games? (2) How are level-0 behaviors perceived by others? We hypothesize that the alignment between level-0 actions and beliefs is context-dependent and that deviations from standard level-0 assumptions can be systematically observed, depending on game features such as payoff structure and the presence of salient focal points.\n\n**Methods: Intra-Team Communication and Data Collection**\n\nTo address these questions, we apply the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), which elicits both actions and beliefs from groups of participants. Teams of three to five participants were randomly assigned and instructed to discuss each game until they reached a consensus on two points: (a) the action they would take as a level-0 player, and (b) their belief about what a generic level-0 player would do. Individual initial responses were recorded before discussion. Group consensus was reached through open discussion, and if unanimity was not possible after a maximum of 10 minutes, a majority rule was applied to determine the team\u2019s final answer. No facilitators were present during discussions, but experimenters monitored for procedural compliance.\n\nAll group discussions were audio-recorded and subsequently transcribed. For qualitative analysis, two independent coders reviewed the transcripts to identify recurring themes in the reasoning process. Coding criteria were developed iteratively, focusing on references to heuristics (e.g., \u201cpick the lowest,\u201d \u201cpick the highest,\u201d \u201cchoose randomly\u201d), salience, and explicit rejection of randomization. Inter-rater reliability was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa (\u03ba = 0.81), indicating substantial agreement. Discrepancies were resolved through discussion.\n\n**Results: Beauty Contest Game**\n\nIn the canonical beauty contest game, where players choose numbers in an attempt to guess a fraction of the average choice, Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) found that elicited level-0 beliefs closely match observed level-0 actions, supporting the standard assumption that level-0 players choose randomly or use salient focal points. Our study replicates this result: in our sample (N = 60 teams), 87% of elicited level-0 beliefs matched the corresponding level-0 actions (95% CI: 78%\u201393%). Both clustered around the salient focal point of zero, consistent with the standard model. We used a chi-squared test to assess whether the observed alignment exceeded what would be expected by chance (\u03c7\u00b2(1, N = 60) = 34.2, p < 0.001). The effect size (Cramer\u2019s V = 0.76) indicates a strong association. This strong alignment reinforces the validity of the traditional level-0 assumption in this context and suggests that, when a salient focal point is present and the payoff structure is simple, participants\u2019 own non-strategic choices and their beliefs about others\u2019 non-strategic choices converge.\n\n**Comparative Context and Results: 11-20 Game Variant**\n\nWhile the beauty contest game demonstrates strong alignment between level-0 actions and beliefs, the pattern differs in other strategic settings. We study a variant of the 11-20 game, originally introduced by Goeree et al. (2018), in which players select integers from 11 to 20 and the highest unique number wins a prize. Our variant modifies the payoff structure by doubling the reward for choosing the highest unique number (from 10 to 20 points), thereby increasing the incentive for strategic reasoning and making the stakes for successful level-k reasoning more pronounced. Goeree et al. (2018) observed that behavior in this game often deviates from standard level-k predictions, with players not following the iterative reasoning process assumed by the model. In our experiment (N = 60 teams), only 54% of elicited level-0 beliefs matched observed level-0 actions (95% CI: 41%\u201366%), and both were widely dispersed across the available choices rather than clustering on a single focal point. A chi-squared test (\u03c7\u00b2(1, N = 60) = 2.13, p = 0.14) indicated that this alignment was not significantly different from chance (Cramer\u2019s V = 0.19, a small effect). This divergence from the standard level-0 assumption suggests that participants may use alternative heuristics or focal points not captured by the traditional model, such as favoring the endpoints or avoiding the most obvious choices. The lack of a salient focal point and the more complex payoff structure likely contribute to this heterogeneity.\n\nQualitative analysis of team discussions revealed several recurring themes. Many teams debated whether a level-0 player would \u201cpick the lowest\u201d (e.g., 11), \u201cpick the highest\u201d (e.g., 20), or \u201cchoose randomly.\u201d Some teams explicitly rejected randomization as unrealistic, with one participant stating, \u201cNo one just picks at random; people look for something that stands out.\u201d Others argued for the salience of endpoints: \u201cIf you don\u2019t think, you just go for the biggest number.\u201d These discussions indicate that teams often considered multiple plausible level-0 strategies and that consensus was sometimes difficult to reach, reflecting heterogeneity in level-0 reasoning. Our findings thus extend the results of Goeree et al. (2018) by showing that heterogeneity in level-0 reasoning, rather than just higher-level strategic thinking, can explain the observed behavioral patterns. This is consistent with alternative models of level-0 behavior that allow for context-dependent heuristics, such as the \u201csalience-based\u201d approach of Crawford et al. (2013), which posits that players use prominent features of the game to guide non-strategic choices, and the \u201cfeature-based\u201d model of Wright & Leyton-Brown (2014), which predicts level-0 behavior from game structure and observed regularities. Our results suggest that the cognitive hierarchy model (Camerer et al., 2004) and quantal response equilibrium (McKelvey & Palfrey, 1995) may benefit from incorporating empirically observed heterogeneity in level-0 behavior, especially in games lacking clear focal points.\n\n**Summary Table: Alignment of Level-0 Actions and Beliefs**\n\n| Game                | % Alignment (Actions = Beliefs) | 95% CI      | Effect Size (Cramer\u2019s V) | Focal Point Present? | Alignment Significance |\n|---------------------|-------------------------------|-------------|-------------------------|----------------------|-----------------------|\n| Beauty Contest      | 87%                           | 78%\u201393%     | 0.76                    | Yes                  | p < 0.001             |\n| 11-20 Game Variant  | 54%                           | 41%\u201366%     | 0.19                    | No                   | p = 0.14              |\n\n**Discussion and Implications**\n\nOur results demonstrate that the correspondence between level-0 beliefs and actions is highly context-dependent: it holds in the beauty contest game but breaks down in the 11-20 game variant. This challenges the assumption that level-0 behavior is stable across games and highlights the need for level-k models to incorporate game-specific factors, such as the salience of focal points, payoff structures, and the complexity of available choices. The presence of a salient focal point (as in the beauty contest) appears to facilitate alignment, while its absence (as in the 11-20 game) leads to greater heterogeneity and weaker alignment. These findings suggest that future models should allow for flexible specifications of level-0 behavior that can be empirically calibrated for each game, for example by incorporating observed distributions of level-0 choices or by allowing for multiple candidate heuristics whose weights are estimated from data, as in the cognitive hierarchy model or the quantal response equilibrium framework.\n\nSeveral limitations of our study should be noted. Our sample, while sufficient for statistical analysis, may not be representative of broader populations. The group discussion format could introduce experimenter demand effects or social conformity pressures, and the absence of facilitators means that some discussions may have been dominated by more vocal participants, potentially biasing consensus outcomes. To address these limitations, future research could employ more diverse sampling strategies, compare group versus individual elicitation, or introduce neutral facilitators to balance participation. Additionally, further studies could systematically vary game features to isolate the effects of focal points and payoff complexity on level-0 reasoning.\n\nIn summary, our study shows that identifying level-0 reasoning requires careful consideration of both beliefs and actions, and that deviations from standard assumptions are informative about the heuristics players use. By clarifying the distinction between level-0 actions and beliefs, providing methodological transparency, integrating qualitative insights, and visually summarizing context-dependent differences, we contribute to the refinement of behavioral models and the improvement of predictions in experimental and applied settings."}}